    [38;5;153msearch          disk-space        91% used[39m
    [38;5;153msearch          index-freshness   last update 47 min ago[39m

  [1mC: a HEALTH column, with each failing check's detail indented under its service's row[0m
  [38;5;153mSERVICE         VERSION   READY   DEPLOYED           HEALTH[39m
  [38;5;153mbilling         4.2.3     2/3     2026-09-28 17:40   1 failing[39m
      [38;5;153mdb-connection: timeout after 5s[39m
  [38;5;153msearch          1.12.0    2/3     2026-09-29 10:10   2 failing[39m
      [38;5;153mdisk-space: 91% used[39m
      [38;5;153mindex-freshness: last update 47 min ago[39m

  [1mWhat actually differs:[0m how much of the failure you see at a glance, and whether the table stays one row per service.
  - [1mA[0m keeps one row per service, which is easiest to [38;5;153mgrep[39m. But it drops the [38;5;153mdetail[39m text, and rows get wide when a
    service has several failing checks.
  - [1mB[0m keeps the table narrow and one row per service, and still shows every detail. The cost is a second block to read.
    Its two parts can change independently later.
  - [1mC[0m keeps everything about a service together. But it breaks one-row-per-service, which hurts [38;5;153mgrep[39m/[38;5;153mawk[39m, and the
    existing "keeps the snapshot order" test assumes that layout.

  All three are cheap to change later, since this is output only. The exception is if someone starts scripting against
  the format.

  [1mMy recommendation: B.[0m The [38;5;153mdetail[39m text ("91% used", "timeout after 5s") is what tells you what to do next, so it should
  show up. B shows it without making the table wider or breaking the one-row-per-service layout.
